@POS
.
=POS
.
.POS(0) gives the cursor column as a UBYTE, as in Commodore BASIC. POS(1)
.gives the cursor row as a UBYTE. Both count from 0. POS(0) = v moves the
.cursor to column v and keeps the row. POS(1) = v moves it to row v and keeps
.the column. v is any expression and is used as a UBYTE. The number in the
.brackets must be written as 0 or 1; anything else is the compile error "POS
.NEEDS 0 OR 1".
.
:DIM C AS UBYTE
:PRINT "ABC";
:C = POS(0)
:PRINT C
:POS(0) = C * 3 + 1
:PRINT POS(0)
.
.This prints 3 after ABC, then 10 starting at column 10.
